SUMMARY:Feather Fest 2026 | Day 2
DESCRIPTION:Have you ever seen or heard\nA poem as lovely as a bird?\n \nA celebration of the wonder and allure of birds\, this two-day festival is made for curious minds of all ages. Storytelling sessions\, workshops\, talks\, and competitions invite everyone to peek into the feathered world and let imagination take flight! \nThis is the second edition of “Feather Fest”\, presented by Early Bird as part of Young Birders’ Month\, a nationwide celebration of birds for young people. \nSchedule \n11am – 12:30pm | Observe\, Sketch\, Record – Building Your Nature Journal (For Ages 9-18) | Library\nwith Subath Senan & WWF India\nDiscover the art of bird observation! Combine basic sketching\, behaviour tracking\, and field notes to create a personal record of nature.\n \n1:30pm – 2:30pm | Games Galore (For Ages 10-13) | Library\nwith Early Bird Team\nTest your memory by matching birds by habitat and food\, pick up fun facts\, and learn calls of common birds around us. Try to survive as a bird in the Bird Survivor and Life Cycle game.\n \n3pm – 4pm | Chirpy Tales – Storytime for children and parents (For Ages 6+) | Library\nwith Vikram Sridhar\nSpread your wings and travel across Bangalore with a storyteller who learnt about Karnataka’s lesser-known birds by waking up early on weekends\, running behind a car\, climbing a tree\, and more. From Black Kites and Indian Rollers to Great White Pelicans\, the journey crosses lakes\, trees\, and bushes. There are new names to learn and a little more of our ecosystem to know!\n \n4pm – 6pm | Moving Feathers – Learn More about Birds through Dance & Movement (For Ages 4-8) | Amphitheatre\nwith Veena Basavarajaiah\nThrough body movement\, children embody different bird behaviours and discover more about our feathered friends\, building curiosity and appreciation along the way. Parents are welcome to accompany and watch. Comfortable clothes that allow free movement are best. Get ready to flap\, hop\, and glide!  \n5pm – 6:30pm | A Pocketful of Birds – A Zine-making Workshop (For Ages 10-13) | Library\nwith Mohit Mahato\nThis session explores the world of birds through drawing\, observation\, and visual storytelling. Children turn their observations\, thoughts\, and experiences into a small handmade zine (booklet)\, using simple techniques and plenty of room to experiment. No experience in drawing or zine-making is needed. What story will you tell?\n \n5:30pm – 6:30pm | A Life Among Birds – Fifty Years of Watching and Wonder (For Ages 14+) | Gallery\nwith S.Subramanya & Garima Bhatia\nA veteran ornithologist looks back on the journeys and stories that shaped a lifelong career with birds\, from tracking migrations to retracing the historic bird survey of Mysore State by Salim Ali. Drawing on decades of research and fieldwork\, the conversation reveals how scientists study birds\, solve mysteries\, and uncover connections between birds and the world around them. Birds are fascinating in themselves\, but the questions they lead to can be even more intriguing.\n \nAcross the Weekend | 11am – 7pm \nLet’s Go Birding through Philately | Board Room\nStep into a world where birdwatching meets philately in this unique exhibition curated by passionate stamp collector Ramu M. Srinivasa. Featuring a selection from his vast collection\, the exhibit showcases bird species across various global themes and taxonomy. The exhibition includes postal materials from the 18th century onwards. \nMeasure your Wingspan | Second Floor Foyer\nWingspan is the distance from fingertip to fingertip with your arms stretched out; and it’s the same for birds! To find yours\, stretch your arms in a T and compare to that of birds found around Bangalore.\n \nWhere Islands Take Flight – A Photo Exhibition | Second Floor Foyer\nAcross 836 islands and islets\, the Andaman and Nicobar archipelago is home to 28 endemic bird species found nowhere else in the world. This exhibition features photographs of endemics\, from the Nicobar megapode as it stirs at dawn to the Nicobar scops owl as its bright yellow eyes glow at night. Each bird is a small story of how life adapts to island homes\, and a reminder of why such fragile places deserve protection. This exhibit is curated by Roundglass Sustain\, an initiative of the Roundglass Foundation dedicated to raising awareness and supporting conservation in India’s natural world through evocative\, science-backed storytelling.\n \nWings of Wonder – An Exhibition on Raptors | First Floor Foyer\nEagles\, kites\, falcons\, and owls sit at the top of the food chain\, and their presence signals a healthy ecosystem. Protecting raptors helps safeguard many other species too. This exhibition by WWF-India opens a window onto the magnificent world of these hunters of the sky\, and shows why their conservation matters to every living thing beneath their wings.\n \nFlights of Imagination – An Art Exhibition | First Floor Foyer\nYoung bird lovers from across the country take flight with creativity in this vibrant showcase of the best entries from a national art competition. Hand-made artworks by children aged 11 to 16 celebrate the beauty and diversity of India’s migratory birds\, seen through youthful eyes. Each piece carries a little of the journey those birds make\, and a lot of the imagination of the artists who painted them. \nFeather Fest Photo Booth | First Floor Foyer\nA cheerful corner for festival memories\, whether posing solo or with family and friends. Bird-inspired outfits are always in the spirit of the day\, and a table of fun bird masks beside the booth adds a flutter of feathers to any look. Strike a pose\, show off your best avian style\, and let your colours shine!\n \nFind the schedule for Day 1 here! \nIn collaboration with: \n                   \n  \n\n\nCelebrating Birds Through Stories\, Art and Beyond\nRSVP here (https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdapNZCKcgKVAqkb-qNXZWqvsZR2dL1QvLUaG0zBCLKtVFlDw/viewform?usp=dialog)\n*Facilitators*: Subath Senan\, Vikram Rajini Sridhar\, Veena Basavarajaiah\, Mohit Mahato\, S. Subramanya\, Garima Bhatia\, Ramu M. Srinivasa\n*Event Categories*: Wildlife\, Workshops\, Young BIC
URL:https://bangaloreinternationalcentre.org/event/feather-fest-2026-day-2/
LOCATION:Bangalore International Centre\, 7\, 4th Main Road\, Domlur II Stage\, Bangalore\, Karnataka\, 560071\, India
CATEGORIES:Wildlife,Workshops,Young BIC
