SUMMARY:Dreamers\, Planners\, Disrupters
DESCRIPTION:\nThis discussion traces India’s journey from colony to independent nation state through the economic debates that came before its policies. Do the arguments of the past still hold relevance as India finds itself at another turning point? \nBased on the book The Ideas That Shaped Modern India and Its Economy\, the discussion looks at three kinds of thinkers: the dreamers who imagined a self-reliant nation\, the planners who tried to engineer it\, and the disrupters who opened India to the world. It will revisit their positions on economic development\, industrialisation\, global trade\, protectionism\, social inclusion\, and job creation. \nVenue: Seminar Halls\n\n\n\nThe Story of Indian Economics\nRSVP here (https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSegiZoVL4FcdcVI8nuFG86FPuEo5Zf8TYXaygZBcNK1nLuBWw/viewform?usp=publish-editor)\n*Speakers*: Niranjan Rajadhyaksha\, Arjun Jayadev\n*Event Categories*: Books\, Development\, Economics\, Governance\, Nonfiction\, Policy & Regulation\, Society
URL:https://bangaloreinternationalcentre.org/event/dreamers-planners-disrupters/
LOCATION:Bangalore International Centre\, 7\, 4th Main Road\, Domlur II Stage\, Bangalore\, Karnataka\, 560071\, India
CATEGORIES:Books,Development,Economics,Governance,Nonfiction,Policy & Regulation,Society
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://bangaloreinternationalcentre.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Thumbnail_DPD2.jpg
END:VEVENT
