SUMMARY:The Sacred and the Erotic in Indian Art
DESCRIPTION:This lecture series considers the erotic as a creative power in visual and built art across India. The three lectures explore how artists connect that power to the sacred and the human. What then becomes possible? \nThe erotic is a resource\, and artists have used it to charge their work. Drawing on images\, sculptures\, architecture\, and literature\, the lectures raise questions about histories\, bodies\, and emotions. Each session begins with an hour-long presentation\, followed by an audience Q&A. \nDec 4 | 6:30 pm – 8 pm | The Connecting Gaze or Touching without Touching\nRani Rupmati and Baz Bahadur\, Radha and Krishna\, a Setupati and his consort\, a Maharana and his friend\, a poet and the gods who receive his devotion and art: all have been painted with their eyes locked. How may we think about these charged acts of gazing into the eyes of another? What is transformed\, exchanged or created? \nDec 5 | 11:30 am – 1 pm | Whose Erotic? The Artist’s\, the Patron’s or the Viewer’s?\nArtists created erotic images for patrons that were meant for them initially but also circulated among other viewers. The image could be of the gods or of a king and a courtesan whose relationship is sacralized through the artistic object. Whose erotic was being shown through the artist’s knowledge and skill? How were these images received \nDec 6 | 11:30 am – 1 pm | The Secular Erotic is the National Sacred\nAfter Independence\, magazine covers\, stamps\, novels\, advertisements\, and films carried images of female dancers and dancing couples. From temple sculptures to Ajanta murals\, Rajput paintings to high modern art\, these cutouts evoke a past where sexuality\, pleasure and the religious were closely tied. How did these joyous dancers sacralise the nation\, and who decided eroticism should promote libidinal investment in it? \nThis series is made possible with the library collections of Art\, Resource and Teaching Trust. \nIn collaboration with: \n \nVenue: Gallery\n\n\nDesire\, Divinity and Art\nRSVP here (https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSeYajKQZvOpFRPBXMCK7sfLpuDBezZBwX1johBASt_Mc-_P9w/viewform?usp=publish-editor)\n4 Dec · 6:30PM – 8:00PM | 5 & 6 Dec · 11:30AM – 1:00PM\n*Speaker*: Annapurna Garimella\n*Event Categories*: Culture\, Heritage\, Society
URL:https://bangaloreinternationalcentre.org/event/the-erotic-the-sacred-and-art-in-india/
LOCATION:Bangalore International Centre\, 7\, 4th Main Road\, Domlur II Stage\, Bangalore\, Karnataka\, 560071\, India
CATEGORIES:Culture,Heritage,Society
