UID:98334-1796342400-1797206399@bangaloreinternationalcentre.org
SUMMARY:Bengaluru Jatre 2026
DESCRIPTION:Bengaluru Jatre is a Government of Karnataka initiative\, led by the Chief Minister and spearheaded by Bengaluru Development Minister Shri Krishna Byre Gowda\, with support from city government agencies. \nMany People. Multiple Cultures. One City. \nAcross Karnataka\, every village and town has its Oora Habba or Jatre\, a joyous celebration that brings people of all ages and backgrounds together around life\, culture\, food\, traditions and community. Even when everyday life is full of challenges\, a Jatre creates an opportunity to pause\, celebrate\, connect and find joy together. Bengaluru Jatre brings this spirit to our city. It is envisioned as an annual\, city-wide celebration through which Bengaluru comes together to celebrate itself\, its people\, neighbourhoods\, cultures\, food\, institutions\, creativity\, history and shared civic identity. \nBIC is happy to be part of the Bengaluru Jatre. \nSchedule \nThe Sacred and the Erotic in India Art | Annapurna Garimella\nDec 4-6 | Lectures\nThe erotic is a resource and artists in India have used it to charge their work with power. Over the course of 3 lectures\, art historian Annapurna Garimella will explore how the erotic is creative power in visual and built art\, how artists connect it to the sacred and what this connection has made possible. The lectures will be given in English with some Telugu and Hindi poetry included. Each lecture will be approximately a 1 hour presentation with 30 minutes for Q & A. \nMast Mahila Mandali | Shilpi Gulati\, Anjum Shaikh & Sheetal Nawle\nDec 5 | 11:30AM | Screening\nTen women from the eastern suburbs of Mumbai sign up for a filmmaking workshop. Not even knowing where the record button is\, they plunge into a messy\, immersive process of making a film together. Stealing time away from family responsibilities and daily chores\, they turn the camera on themselves. They capture life of the city’s working-class\, while talking about husbands\, murders\, toilets\, and everything in between. Evolved from an 18-month process in co-creation\, Mast Mahila Mandali is a riotous\, plotless\, genre-bending documentary about how women labour\, love\, and claim joy in everyday life.\nRSVP here \nCradle to Grave: Welfare in India | Reetika Khera & Aakar Patel\nDec 5 | 6:30PM | Discussion\nBased on the book Freebies or Rights\, this discussion examines how social policy actually shapes everyday life: from school meals and the Public Distribution System to maternity benefits and the rural employment guarantee. Is state-provided support simply a set of “freebies” or is it the right that makes a dignified life possible?\nRSVP here \nCultivating Organisational Longevity | Subramanian Kalpathi\, Pranay Jivrajka\, Vishal Talreja & Aruna Gopakumar\nDec 6 | 6:00PM | Discussion\nModern workplaces pursue growth\, agility and performance\, yet many unintentionally create conditions that exhaust their people and weaken their capacity to endure. Burnout\, toxic cultures\, unsustainable ways of working\, outdated work models and depleted leaders can reinforce one another\, gradually shortening an organisation’s healthy lifespan. This event will examine what current research reveals about burnout\, workplace culture and sustainable performance.\nRSVP here \nRemaking the Indian Citizen | Yamini Aiyar\nDec 6 | 6:30PM | Lecture\nThis lecture examines how the democratic boundaries of citizenship in India are shifting\, from voting to welfare delivery\, asking what the changes mean for the constitutional promise of equal citizenship. It makes the case that routine bureaucratic processes are being used to create a new hierarchy of citizenship\, reshaping what it means to be an Indian citizen. Who counts as a citizen? Who decides? \nSmart Machines\, Wise Leaders | Swati Apte\, Garima Deveshwar Bahl & Shrikant Karwa\nDec 7 | 6:30PM | Discussion\nBased on the book The Unartificial Leader\, this discussion asks what humans must hold onto as machines grow more capable. Drawing on interviews with CEOs\, founders\, and next-generation leaders\, the authors argue that intelligence and wisdom are not the same thing.\nRSVP here \nBangalore at a Crossroads | Supriya Baily\nDec 8 | 6:30PM | Lecture\nIn this talk\, Supriya Baily explores one of India’s most dynamic cities\, Bangalore\, through the eyes of a group of women who grew up and went to school together in the late 1980s and early 1990s. Through the stories of school girls in what used to be one of India’s most progressive city\, Bangalore Girls reveals how the freedom women once enjoyed has been eroded by the rising tide of right-wing nationalism\, misogyny\, and religious fundamentalism. \nArt\, Madness and Memory | Maitreyee B. Chowdhury & Shamya Dasgupta\nDec 9 | 6:30PM | Discussion\nAn epistolary novel set in 1969\, takes into account the fact that two geniuses\, film maker Ritwik Ghatak and Poet Binoy Majumdar find themselves within the same mental asylum. As they discuss their lives\, secrets are revealed\, dark truths brought to light\, and a play is performed. What happens next? \nSix Degrees of Infatuation | Geeta Chandran\nDec 10 | 7:00PM | Performance\nSix Degrees of Infatuation\, a solo performance by Geeta Chandran\, explores the varied dimensions of infatuation\, love and adoration that spans the realms of both the divine and the human\, drawing from sources as varied as Sanskrit poetry\, Vernacular verses\, the Ramayana and the vast cornucopia of Indian devotional poetry. \nIn collaboration with: \n \n\n\nMany People. Multiple Cultures. One City\n*Event Categories*: Bangalore\, Culture
URL:https://bangaloreinternationalcentre.org/event/bengaluru-jatre-2026/
LOCATION:Bangalore International Centre\, 7\, 4th Main Road\, Domlur II Stage\, Bangalore\, Karnataka\, 560071\, India
CATEGORIES:Bangalore,Culture
